//RS 
//RS /// Declare that the program about to run owns the process's stdout and stderr,
//RS /// so both in-process tiers write its output through as it is produced instead
//RS /// of accumulating it for a caller that reads `RunOutcome` as data. The one-shot
//RS /// `jet run` / `jet dev` verbs call this: they only re-print that buffer, and
//RS /// withholding it made a piped program that prints and keeps running — a server,
//RS /// a watch loop, anything with a `loop` — print nothing until it ended, while an
//RS /// AOT build of the same source printed immediately. The fact itself, its name,
//RS /// and the routing rule live in `Prelude/Term.rs` (I8/I9).
